Since the doubly linked list is sorted, all duplicate values appear adjacent to each other.
So instead of checking every node globally, we only need to compare each node with its next node.

The idea is to keep one pointer at the current node and:

- If the next node has the same value → skip it
- Otherwise → move forward

This way, we retain only one instance of each value.

## Approach
Initialize two pointers:
left → points to the last unique node
right → scans the list
Traverse the list:
If left.val != right.val:
Link left.next to right
Update right.prev to left
Move left forward
Else:
Skip the duplicate by moving right
After traversal, set left.next = null to remove trailing duplicates

